            "[[chorea|Chorea]] has many causes, so look at age, tempo, family history, and drugs.\n\n[[huntington-disease|Huntington disease]] is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disease caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin (HTT) gene. It typically begins in adult life with chorea, psychiatric symptoms (depression, irritability, apathy), and cognitive decline, especially in planning and organization. A parent with a similar illness, sometimes misdiagnosed or dying young, is a strong clue. Genetic testing confirms the diagnosis, and because the result affects the whole family, it is done with genetic counseling and consent.\n\nOther causes of chorea to consider include drugs (dopaminergic treatment in Parkinson disease, where it appears as levodopa-induced [[dyskinesia|dyskinesia]]), metabolic causes such as hyperglycemia, autoimmune causes, pregnancy, and post-streptococcal Sydenham chorea in children.\n\n[[dystonia|Dystonia]] is recognized by its pattern: the same muscles pull the same way each time, often worse with a particular action (writer's cramp) and often eased by a light touch to the area, the [[sensory-trick|sensory trick]]. Describe it by age at onset and distribution: focal (one body region, such as cervical dystonia twisting the neck), segmental, multifocal, generalized, or hemidystonia. Onset in childhood, generalized spread, or hemidystonia make an underlying cause more likely and need investigation.",
